    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d767">

                <pb facs="0668" n="659" xml:id="pg659"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 Defense (M)/195: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-08-10T16:00:00-04:00">August 10, 1940—4
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>140. The American Embassy at Vichy (Paris) has been informed by Wellington Koo
                    that “according to Chungking the Soviet Union has waived claim to the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Selenga</hi> cargo” and reports further that Koo
                    “understood that Moscow would instruct the Soviet Chargé at Vichy accordingly”.
                    French officials have advised the American Embassy that if this is the
                    situation, the French Government will give orders for the immediate release of
                    the ores in question.</p>
                <p>The Department has not yet been informed however that the Soviet Government has
                    taken any action on the matter and believes it possible that Wellington Koo was
                    misinformed or misunderstood his instructions. Your 382, August 6, 5
                        p.m.,<anchor n="35" xml:id="d767-f1"/> does not indicate that the matter has
                    been cleared with the Moscow Government by the Chungking Government. Please
                    check with the Chinese authorities, report to the Department, and secure if
                    desirable clarifying instructions to Wellington Koo.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Welles</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
